Subject: [PATCH] ios-m0: share one read-notification pipe per thread in the Mach transport

Mach ports are not pollable, so a transport's Mach IO thread cannot hand messages to the
owning thread's poll-based Core::EventLoop directly. It writes a byte to a pipe the event
loop watches. That pipe was allocated in the TransportMachPort constructor, which means
every new IPC connection called pipe().

That is a problem on iOS, because the only useful precompiled kernel sandbox profile
(`com.apple.WebKit.WebContent`) denies pipe(), and the call is wrapped in MUST(), so a
denial aborts. Measured on device: pipe() is EPERM under that profile while Mach port
allocation, port sets, and threads all still work. Transports get created long after
startup -- WebContent attaches to RequestServer/ImageDecoder/Compositor from inside the
event loop, ImageDecoder builds one per new client, and MessagePort::entangle_with builds
a pair for every `new MessageChannel()` in page JavaScript -- so there is no call site
where confinement could be applied before the last pipe() call. Full matrix and reasoning
in docs/ladybird-sandbox-confinement.md.

The pipe is only a "come look" doorbell, though. It carries no per-transport data: the
actual state is m_read_notification_pending, already coalesced under m_incoming_mutex. So
one pipe per thread is enough. The first transport on a thread creates it, every later
transport reuses it, and dispatch scans the thread's registered transports for pending
flags instead of relying on which fd woke up.

Ordering is what makes this safe, and it is easy to get wrong. The channel is joined in the
transport constructor, before the IO thread starts, so a doorbell always has a pipe to land
in even for a message that arrives before any read hook exists. An earlier revision of this
patch created the channel in set_up_read_hook() instead, which lost the first message on
every connection: the IO thread would set the pending flag with no channel to ring, and the
catch-up path then asked schedule_read_notification_if_needed_locked() whether to ring, got
"already pending, someone else will do it", and nobody ever did. WebContent hung on startup.
The catch-up in set_up_read_hook() therefore rings unconditionally rather than consulting
that helper.

Beyond that: a transport sets its pending flag under the mutex and only then writes its byte,
and dispatch drains the pipe before scanning, so a transport that becomes pending after the
drain has already written a byte and earns another activation rather than being lost. Extra
bytes are harmless because dispatch always scans every transport. Core::Notifier needs a
running event loop while the pipe does not, so the notifier is created lazily by
set_up_read_hook() and the pipe eagerly by the constructor.

The registry is mutex-guarded. Transports are expected to be created and destroyed on their
owning thread, but nothing enforces that, and a torn Vector would be a miserable bug to
chase. The lock is never held while a read hook runs, because a hook can create or destroy
transports and that would deadlock.

This is worth doing on its own terms: it removes two file descriptors per IPC connection.
It also means a process confined after its first transport exists never calls pipe() again,
which is what makes the sandbox patch that follows possible.
